docs/nitro-sandboxes/README.md:78:| **Confidential mode** | The default mode. The parent has no access to the sandbox's memory, device I/O goes through DMBs, and the sandbox can attest what it runs. For more about the two modes, see [Memory modes](how-it-works.md#memory-modes).                                                                                                                                                                   |
docs/nitro-sandboxes/README.md:79:| **Direct mode**       | The mode where the parent keeps read/write access to the sandbox's memory. It has no attestation, and the guest does not need the DMB virtio drivers. For more about the two modes, see [Memory modes](how-it-works.md#memory-modes).                                                                                                                                                        |
docs/nitro-sandboxes/README.md:80:| **Enclave**           | The term the Firecracker API and code base use for a sandbox, because sandboxes are built on Nitro Enclaves. The configuration endpoint is `PUT /enclave`, its errors name enclaves, and the kernel interface is `/dev/nitro_enclaves`. Read it as a synonym for sandbox throughout. It is not the same thing as an AWS Nitro Enclave, which has a single vsock channel and no device model. |
docs/nitro-sandboxes/README.md:82:| **EIF**               | Enclave Image Format, the measured boot artifact a sandbox boots from. For its contents and the two ways to obtain one, see [Booting](how-it-works.md#booting).                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |
docs/nitro-sandboxes/README.md:83:| **DMB**               | Device Memory Buffer, a per-device memory region shared between the guest and the device backend in the parent, in which that device's virtqueues live. Confidential mode uses one per device, sized by its `dmb_size_mib`. For more about sizing DMBs, see [DMB size](features.md#dmb-size).                                                                                                |
